<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colonial Office</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colonial Office was the British government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> that administered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s colonies, separated from the War Office in 1854. Crown colonies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> were governed by officials who reported to the Secretary of State for the Colonies. It took over territories such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a> and, after company rule ended, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, and was merged into the Commonwealth Office in 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colonial-office.jpg" alt="Colonial Office" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illustrated London News · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foreign_and_India_Offices,_London,_1866_ILN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1911 the company joined its two territories into Northern Rhodesia, which passed to the British <b>Colonial Office</b> in 1924.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sixth Frontier War of 1834–1836 led Governor Benjamin D’Urban to annex the Province of Queen Adelaide, an annexation reversed by the <b>Colonial Office</b> in 1836.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Colonial Office</b>'s statement of 1922 said Palestine as a whole was not to become a Jewish national home, a reading the Zionist Executive then accepted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top stood a metropolitan authority: Spain’s Council of the Indies from 1524, Britain’s <b>Colonial Office</b>, France’s ministry of the colonies, or a chartered company such as the English East India Company (1600) or the Dutch East India Company (1602), which could make war and treaties in…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>In a 1922 referendum they rejected union with South Africa, and in 1923 Southern Rhodesia became a self-governing British colony; Northern Rhodesia passed to the <b>Colonial Office</b> in 1924, though the company kept mineral rights.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>It was governed by the Foreign Office rather than the <b>Colonial Office</b>, and its officials began pushing into the hinterland of the Niger Delta.</blockquote><a href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
